U3.mc = [
['foundations','Which statement describes a saturated solution in contact with excess solid at equilibrium?', ['Dissolving has stopped.','Dissolving and crystallization occur at equal rates.','Dissolved and undissolved masses are equal.','Every ion has the same concentration.'],1,'Equilibrium is dynamic: both processes continue at equal rates. Neither equal masses nor equal ion concentrations is required.','Package 1'],
['foundations','Which solute forms a molecular solution in water under ordinary classroom conditions?', ['NaCl','NH₄NO₃','CH₃OH','K₂SO₄'],2,'Methanol remains as neutral CH₃OH molecules. The other three substances are soluble ionic salts.','Package 1'],
['foundations','A clear solution is poured off a saturated salt mixture without changing its temperature. What can you conclude?', ['It must be unsaturated because no solid is visible.','Its solubility doubles.','Its concentration falls to zero.','It remains saturated if its composition is unchanged.'],3,'The dissolved concentration remains at the saturation limit. A visible solid is not necessary for the decanted liquid to retain that concentration.','Hebden #6'],
['ions','What are [Al³⁺] and [SO₄²⁻] in 0.150 M Al₂(SO₄)₃?', ['0.300 M and 0.450 M','0.150 M and 0.150 M','0.450 M and 0.300 M','0.0750 M and 0.0500 M'],0,'Al₂(SO₄)₃ supplies two Al³⁺ and three SO₄²⁻ per formula unit. Multiply 0.150 M by 2 and 3.','Package 4'],
['dilution','25.0 mL of 0.200 M NaCl is mixed with 75.0 mL water. What is [Cl⁻]?', ['0.200 M','0.0667 M','0.0500 M','0.800 M'],2,'The total volume is 100.0 mL. [Cl⁻] = 0.200 × 25.0/100.0 = 0.0500 M.','Package 3'],
['equations','Which pair forms a precipitate when equal volumes of 0.20 M solutions are mixed?', ['KNO₃ and NaCl','Mg(NO₃)₂ and KCl','NH₄Cl and NaNO₃','BaCl₂ and Na₂SO₄'],3,'Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s). The other cross-products are soluble under these conditions.','Package 5'],
['equations','In the complete ionic equation for AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq), which species must stay intact?', ['AgNO₃(aq)','AgCl(s)','NaCl(aq)','NaNO₃(aq)'],1,'Do not split a solid precipitate into aqueous ions. The three soluble salts dissociate in the complete ionic equation.','Package 5'],
['equations','Which is the net ionic equation for precipitating lead(II) iodide?', ['Pb²⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s)','Pb²⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq) → PbI(s)','PbI₂(s) → Pb²⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq)','K⁺(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq) → KNO₃(s)'],0,'The product formula must balance charges: one Pb²⁺ needs two I⁻. Net precipitation puts the solid on the right.','Package 5'],
['ksp','Which expression is correct for Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2Ag⁺ + CrO₄²⁻?', ['[2Ag⁺][CrO₄²⁻]','[Ag⁺][CrO₄²⁻]²','[Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]','[Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]/[Ag₂CrO₄]'],2,'Coefficients become exponents. The pure solid is omitted; do not insert a second stoichiometric factor inside [Ag⁺].','Package 7'],
['ksp','An AB₂ salt has molar solubility s in pure water. What is Ksp?', ['2s²','s²','2s³','4s³'],3,'AB₂ → A + 2B, so [A] = s and [B] = 2s. Ksp = s(2s)² = 4s³.','Package 7'],
['ksp','AgCl has Ksp = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. Its pure-water molar solubility is closest to:', ['1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M','1.3 × 10⁻⁵ M','9.0 × 10⁻¹¹ M','2.7 × 10⁻⁴ M'],1,'AgCl is 1:1, so s = √(1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34 × 10⁻⁵ M.','Package 7'],
['ksp','MgF₂ has Ksp = 6.4 × 10⁻⁹. Which is closest to [F⁻] in pure water?', ['2.3 × 10⁻³ M','1.2 × 10⁻³ M','8.0 × 10⁻⁵ M','6.4 × 10⁻⁹ M'],0,'s = ∛(Ksp/4) = 0.0011696 M; [F⁻] = 2s = 0.002339 M. Keep guard digits before doubling.','Package 7'],
['precip','After dilution, [Ag⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M and [Cl⁻] = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. What happens?', ['No solid; Q < Ksp.','No solid; Q > Ksp.','AgCl precipitates; Q > Ksp.','AgCl precipitates; Q < Ksp.'],2,'Q = (1.0 × 10⁻⁵)(3.0 × 10⁻⁵) = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰, which exceeds Ksp.','Package 8'],
['precip','A mixture has Q exactly equal to Ksp. What is the best description?', ['Always unsaturated.','All dissolved ions disappear.','Ksp changes until a solid forms.','At the saturation / precipitation threshold.'],3,'Equality marks saturation. Your notes describe this as “just starts.” Net precipitation is driven by Q > Ksp.','Package 8'],
['precip','[Pb²⁺] = 0.020 M. For PbI₂, Ksp = 8.5 × 10⁻⁹. What [I⁻] reaches the precipitation threshold?', ['4.3 × 10⁻⁷ M','6.5 × 10⁻⁴ M','1.8 × 10⁻⁵ M','8.5 × 10⁻⁹ M'],1,'[I⁻] = √(Ksp/[Pb²⁺]) = √(4.25 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.52 × 10⁻⁴ M.','Package 8'],
['separation','A solution may contain Ag⁺, Ba²⁺ and Ni²⁺. Which is the best first reagent for separating one cation?', ['NaCl(aq)','Na₂CO₃(aq)','Na₂SO₄(aq)','Na₃PO₄(aq)'],0,'Chloride selectively precipitates AgCl while Ba²⁺ and Ni²⁺ remain dissolved. Filter before the next test.','Package 6'],
['titration','A 10.00 mL chloride sample needs 8.40 mL of 0.100 M AgNO₃. What is [Cl⁻] in this sample?', ['0.00840 M','0.840 M','0.0840 M','0.184 M'],2,'n(Ag⁺) = 0.100 × 0.00840 = 8.40 × 10⁻⁴ mol = n(Cl⁻). Divide by 0.01000 L. No dilution factor is stated.','Package 8 / lab'],
['common','Solid NaBr is added to saturated AgBr in contact with solid, with negligible volume change. At the new equilibrium:', ['Ksp is smaller.','[Ag⁺] and [Br⁻] both decrease overall.','[Ag⁺] increases and [Br⁻] decreases.','[Ag⁺] decreases and [Br⁻] increases overall.'],3,'Adding the common ion Br⁻ promotes precipitation. Ksp is constant; lower [Ag⁺] balances higher [Br⁻].','Package 9'],
['hard','Which soluble reagent can remove both Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ by precipitation under the usual class-table conditions?', ['NaNO₃','Na₂CO₃','NaCl','NaI'],1,'Carbonate forms low-solubility CaCO₃ and MgCO₃. Nitrate, chloride and iodide do not remove these hardness ions this way.','MC: Aug 2000 #20'],
['hard','Which anion can precipitate Ca²⁺ while leaving Mg²⁺ dissolved, when sufficient reagent is added?', ['SO₄²⁻','NO₃⁻','Cl⁻','CO₃²⁻'],0,'CaSO₄ has low solubility but MgSO₄ is soluble. Carbonate can remove both. For actual concentrations, check Q > Ksp.','MC: Aug 1998 #17'],
['foundations','Which is an appropriate unit of molar solubility?', ['g/s','mol/s','mol/L','M/L'],2,'Molar solubility is a concentration: mol/L, also called M. Rates such as g/s do not describe solubility.','Package 1'],
['molar','0.500 g of a salt (molar mass 200.0 g/mol) saturates 250.0 mL of solution. Find s.', ['0.00250 M','0.0100 M','2.00 M','0.125 M'],1,'n = 0.500/200.0 = 0.00250 mol. Divide by 0.2500 L to obtain 0.0100 M.','Package 2'],
['ions','A dissolved Na₂SO₄ solution has [Na⁺] = 0.0600 M. What is [SO₄²⁻]?', ['0.120 M','0.0600 M','0.0150 M','0.0300 M'],3,'Each formula unit makes two sodium ions and one sulfate ion. Sulfate concentration is half the sodium concentration.','Package 4'],
['dilution','50.0 mL of 0.200 M CaCl₂ is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.200 M NaCl. Find [Cl⁻].', ['0.300 M','0.200 M','0.100 M','0.600 M'],0,'Chloride comes from both salts: (2 × 0.200 × 0.0500 + 0.200 × 0.0500)/0.1000 = 0.300 M.','Hebden #20f'],
['equations','Under class-table conditions, mixing ZnSO₄(aq) and Sr(OH)₂(aq) can form:', ['No precipitate.','Only Zn(OH)₂.','Zn(OH)₂ and SrSO₄.','Only SrSO₄.'],2,'Check both cross-combinations. Both products have low solubility at the concentrated conditions in the MC package.','MC: April 1999 #17'],
['equations','Which pair of soluble reactants is suitable for making PbSO₄(s)?', ['PbCl₂(s) and BaSO₄(s)','Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) and Na₂SO₄(aq)','PbSO₄(s) and NaNO₃(aq)','NaNO₃(aq) and KCl(aq)'],1,'Nitrate supplies Pb²⁺ in solution; a sodium salt supplies sulfate. Insoluble solids do not supply the intended high concentrations of free ions.','Hebden #24 skill'],
['ksp','A 1:1 salt has molar solubility 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ M in pure water. Its Ksp is:', ['3.0 × 10⁻⁵','6.0 × 10⁻⁵','9.0 × 10⁻⁵','9.0 × 10⁻¹⁰'],3,'Ksp = s² = (3.0 × 10⁻⁵)² = 9.0 × 10⁻¹⁰. Square both the coefficient and the power of ten.','Package 7'],
['ksp','A saturated Ag₂CrO₄ solution made with pure water has [Ag⁺] = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ M. What is s?', ['2.0 × 10⁻⁴ M','4.0 × 10⁻⁴ M','8.0 × 10⁻⁴ M','1.6 × 10⁻⁷ M'],0,'Each dissolved formula unit makes two silver ions. s = [Ag⁺]/2.','Package 7'],
['ksp','A₃B₂ has molar solubility s in pure water. Which expression equals Ksp?', ['6s²','6s⁵','108s⁵','5s⁶'],2,'[A] = 3s and [B] = 2s. Ksp = (3s)³(2s)² = 27 × 4 × s⁵ = 108s⁵.','Package 7 extension'],
['ksp','Which statement about comparing solubilities is reliable?', ['The smaller Ksp always gives the more soluble salt.','For salts with different ion ratios, calculate s before comparing.','All salts with the same Ksp have the same s.','Ksp and s are interchangeable.'],1,'Different stoichiometries produce different Ksp-to-s equations. Direct Ksp ranking works for the same ion ratio under comparable conditions.','Package 7'],
['precip','Equal volumes of 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ M AgNO₃ and 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ M NaCl are mixed. Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰. Predict the result.', ['Q = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰; precipitate.','Q = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰; precipitate.','Q = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰; no precipitate.','Q = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰; no precipitate.'],3,'Both ion concentrations halve to 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. Q = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ < Ksp, so no precipitate.','Package 8'],
['precip','In Hebden #68 conditions, 0.10 M each Cl⁻, I⁻ and SO₄²⁻ are present. Required [Pb²⁺] values are 1.2 × 10⁻³, 8.5 × 10⁻⁷ and 1.8 × 10⁻⁷ M, respectively. Which forms first?', ['PbSO₄','PbI₂','PbCl₂','All three simultaneously'],0,'The smallest added-ion threshold is reached first: 1.8 × 10⁻⁷ M for PbSO₄.','Hebden #68'],
['separation','After AgCl is precipitated from an Ag⁺/Ba²⁺/Ni²⁺ mixture, what should happen before adding the next reagent?', ['Add more AgNO₃.','Evaporate everything to dryness.','Filter and test the filtrate.','Discard the liquid and test only the solid.'],2,'Filtration removes the identified ion as solid. The remaining candidates are in the filtrate, which is used for the next selective test.','Package 6'],
['titration','What signals the endpoint of the chloride titration with chromate indicator?', ['The first white AgCl cloud.','A persistent faint red/orange tint from Ag₂CrO₄.','A blue copper precipitate.','The disappearance of all solid.'],1,'AgCl forms throughout the titration. After chloride is nearly consumed, excess Ag⁺ forms red silver chromate; the faint colour persists after swirling.','Lab'],
['common','In the school common-ion model, AgCl is least soluble in which solution?', ['Pure water','0.10 M NaNO₃','0.10 M NaCl','0.10 M ZnCl₂'],3,'ZnCl₂ supplies 0.20 M Cl⁻, more common ion than 0.10 M NaCl, suppressing AgCl solubility the most among these choices.','Package 9'],
['common','At fixed temperature, more AgCl(s) is added to a saturated AgCl solution already in contact with excess solid. What changes?', ['The amount of solid, while saturated ion concentrations remain the same.','Ksp doubles.','[Ag⁺] doubles.','The solution becomes unsaturated.'],0,'The amount of pure solid does not enter Ksp. At equilibrium and fixed temperature the saturated concentrations are unchanged.','Package 9'],
['hard','A softener replaces each Ca²⁺ ion with sodium ions. Which replacement preserves charge?', ['One Na⁺','One Na²⁺','Two Na⁺','Two Na²⁺'],2,'One 2+ ion is exchanged for two 1+ ions. Sodium does not cause the calcium/magnesium soap-scum problem.','Hard-water extension'],
['hard','Which statement about water softened with Na₂SO₄ is correct?', ['All Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ must be removed at any concentration.','Mg²⁺ can remain because MgSO₄ is soluble.','Sulfate only removes Na⁺.','No dissolved ions remain.'],1,'Sulfate may precipitate calcium sulfate if Q exceeds Ksp, but magnesium sulfate remains soluble.','Hard water'],
['hard','At the concentrated conditions in June 2002 #19, which anion can precipitate both Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺?', ['Cl⁻','NO₃⁻','I⁻','OH⁻'],3,'Both hydroxides can precipitate with enough OH⁻. This is concentration-dependent, especially for Ca(OH)₂; use Q when concentrations/Ksp are given.','MC: June 2002 #19'],
['hard','Why can hard water reduce the lather produced by soap?', ['Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ form low-solubility salts with soap anions.','Water becomes electrically neutral.','Na⁺ destroys every water molecule.','Hard water contains no dissolved ions.'],0,'Soap is consumed in insoluble calcium/magnesium salts (scum), leaving less available for lathering.','Hard-water extension']
].map((q,i)=>({id:'mc'+(i+1),tag:q[0],q:q[1],a:q[2],correct:q[3],why:q[4],ref:q[5]}));
U3.written=[
{id:'w1',title:'Solubility curves & cooling',marks:8,ref:'Package 1 · Hebden #17 skills',q:`<p>An original practice curve is described by the values below. Assume equilibrium and no evaporation. Solubility is in <b>g salt per 100 g H₂O</b>.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Temperature (°C)</th><th>Salt A</th><th>Salt B</th><th>Salt C</th></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>30</td><td>55</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>40</td><td>70</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>50</td><td>85</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>80</td><td>60</td><td>100</td><td>30</td></tr></table></div><ol type="a" class="spaced"><li>Identify the most soluble salt at 60 °C. (1)</li><li>45 g of A is added to 100 g water at 40 °C. Describe the liquid and find the mass of undissolved solid. (2)</li><li>A saturated solution of A in 150 g water at 80 °C is cooled to 20 °C. Find the mass that crystallizes. (3)</li><li>Explain what happens when saturated C is heated from 20 to 80 °C, and infer the sign of its dissolving enthalpy from this trend. (2)</li></ol>`,solution:`<p><b>(a)</b> B: 85 g/100 g H₂O is the highest at 60 °C.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A’s capacity is 40 g. The liquid is saturated and <b>5 g remains undissolved</b>. It is not a supersaturated solution just because 45 g was added.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Initial dissolved mass = 60 × 150/100 = 90 g. Final dissolved mass = 30 × 150/100 = 45 g. Crystals = 90 − 45 = <b>45 g</b>.</p><p><b>(d)</b> C becomes less soluble on heating, so crystals form. The dissolving process is <b>exothermic</b> in this model: heat favours the solid side.</p><p class="rubric">Marking: (a) correct salt 1; (b) saturated 1 + 5 g 1; (c) initial capacity 1 + final capacity 1 + difference 1; (d) crystallization 1 + exothermic reasoning 1.</p>`},
{id:'w2',title:'Ksp from solubility; solubility from Ksp',marks:10,ref:'Package 7 · #42–55 skills',q:`<ol type="a" class="spaced"><li>A salt MX₂ has molar mass 200.0 g/mol. A maximum of 0.0200 g dissolves to make 100.0 mL of saturated solution. Write its dissolution equation (M²⁺ and X⁻), calculate s, and find Ksp. (5)</li><li>For Ag₂CrO₄, Ksp = 1.1 × 10⁻¹². Construct a pure-water ICE table, find the molar solubility, [Ag⁺], and mass that dissolves in 250.0 mL. Molar mass = 331.7 g/mol. (5)</li></ol>`,solution:`<p><b>(a)</b> MX₂(s) ⇌ M²⁺(aq) + 2X⁻(aq).<br>n = 0.0200/200.0 = 1.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol.<br>s = 1.00 × 10⁻⁴/0.1000 = <b>1.00 × 10⁻³ M</b>.<br>[M²⁺] = s; [X⁻] = 2s = 2.00 × 10⁻³ M.<br>Ksp = [M²⁺][X⁻]² = (1.00 × 10⁻³)(2.00 × 10⁻³)² = <b>4.00 × 10⁻⁹</b>.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq).</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>mol/L</th><th>Ag⁺</th><th>CrO₄²⁻</th></tr><tr><th>I</th><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><th>C</th><td>+2s</td><td>+s</td></tr><tr><th>E</th><td>2s</td><td>s</td></tr></table></div><p>Ksp = (2s)²s = 4s³ = 1.1 × 10⁻¹².<br>s = ∛(1.1 × 10⁻¹² / 4) = 6.50296 × 10⁻⁵ M → <b>6.5 × 10⁻⁵ M</b>.<br>[Ag⁺] = 2s = <b>1.3 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>.<br>Mass = sVMᵣ = (6.50296 × 10⁻⁵)(0.2500)(331.7) = <b>5.4 × 10⁻³ g</b>.</p><p class="rubric">(a) equation 1 + n 1 + s 1 + expression/ion ratios 1 + Ksp 1. (b) equation and ICE 1 + 4s³ setup 1 + s 1 + [Ag⁺] 1 + mass with units 1.</p>`},
{id:'w3',title:'Mixing, Q, and all three equations',marks:12,ref:'Packages 4–5 and 8',q:`<p>Mix 25.0 mL of 4.00 × 10⁻³ M Pb(NO₃)₂ with 75.0 mL of 4.00 × 10⁻³ M KI. Use Ksp(PbI₂) = 8.5 × 10⁻⁹. Assume additive volumes.</p><ol type="a" class="spaced"><li>Find [Pb²⁺] and [I⁻] immediately after mixing, before reaction. (3)</li><li>Calculate Q and predict whether a precipitate forms. (3)</li><li>Write the balanced formula, complete ionic and net ionic precipitation equations, including states. (4)</li><li>Find the spectator-ion concentrations after mixing: [K⁺] and [NO₃⁻]. (2)</li></ol>`,solution:`<p><b>(a)</b> V<sub>total</sub> = 100.0 mL.<br>[Pb²⁺] = (4.00 × 10⁻³)(25.0/100.0) = <b>1.00 × 10⁻³ M</b>.<br>[I⁻] = (4.00 × 10⁻³)(75.0/100.0) = <b>3.00 × 10⁻³ M</b>.</p><p><b>(b)</b> PbI₂(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻, so Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² = (1.00 × 10⁻³)(3.00 × 10⁻³)² = <b>9.00 × 10⁻⁹</b>. Since Q &gt; 8.5 × 10⁻⁹, <b>PbI₂ precipitates</b>. Keep guard digits: these numbers are deliberately close.</p><p><b>(c) Formula:</b> Pb(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → PbI₂(s) + 2KNO₃(aq).</p><p><b>Complete ionic:</b> Pb²⁺(aq) + 2NO₃⁻(aq) + 2K⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s) + 2K⁺(aq) + 2NO₃⁻(aq).</p><p><b>Net ionic:</b> Pb²⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s).</p><p><b>(d)</b> [K⁺] = <b>3.00 × 10⁻³ M</b>. [NO₃⁻] = 2(4.00 × 10⁻³)(25.0/100.0) = <b>2.00 × 10⁻³ M</b>. Spectators are not consumed. The reacting-ion values in (a) are initial mixed concentrations, not the final equilibrium values after precipitation.</p><p class="rubric">(a) total volume 1 + each concentration 1; (b) Q expression 1 + number 1 + comparison/conclusion 1; (c) formula 1 + complete ionic 1 + net ionic 1 + states/charge/balance 1; (d) 1 each.</p>`},
{id:'w4',title:'Chloride titration & dilution',marks:10,ref:'Package 8 + chloride lab',q:`<p>A water sample is diluted 10.0×. A 10.00 mL aliquot of this diluted sample is titrated with 0.100 M AgNO₃ and chromate indicator.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Initial buret (mL)</th><th>Final buret (mL)</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2.10</td><td>10.42</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>10.42</td><td>18.72</td></tr></table></div><ol type="a" class="spaced"><li>Calculate the two titres and their mean. (2)</li><li>Write the chloride precipitation reaction and find the moles of chloride in the aliquot. (2)</li><li>Calculate chloride concentration in the diluted and original samples. (3)</li><li>Name the endpoint precipitate and its visible signal. Explain how overshooting changes the calculated chloride concentration. (3)</li></ol>`,solution:`<p><b>(a)</b> V₁ = 10.42 − 2.10 = <b>8.32 mL</b>; V₂ = 18.72 − 10.42 = <b>8.30 mL</b>; mean = <b>8.31 mL</b>.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s).<br>n(Ag⁺) = 0.100 × 0.00831 = 8.31 × 10⁻⁴ mol.<br>1:1, so n(Cl⁻) = <b>8.31 × 10⁻⁴ mol</b>.</p><p><b>(c)</b> [Cl⁻]<sub>diluted</sub> = 8.31 × 10⁻⁴/0.01000 = <b>0.0831 M</b>.<br>[Cl⁻]<sub>original</sub> = 10.0 × 0.0831 = <b>0.831 M</b>.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Ag₂CrO₄</b> gives a persistent faint <b>red/orange</b> tint. Overshooting makes the titre too large, so n = CV is overestimated and the reported chloride concentration is <b>too high</b>.</p><p class="rubric">(a) titres 1 + mean 1; (b) equation 1 + moles with 1:1 ratio 1; (c) aliquot volume in L 1 + diluted result 1 + dilution correction 1; (d) precipitate 1 + colour 1 + error direction/reason 1.</p>`},
{id:'w5',title:'Identify, separate, and choose the first precipitate',marks:10,ref:'Package 6 + package 8 / Hebden #68',q:`<ol type="a" class="spaced"><li>A solution may contain Ag⁺, Ba²⁺ and Ni²⁺. Describe a sequential procedure using soluble reagents to identify and separate them. Give the net ionic equations and say what happens between tests. (6)</li><li>Separately, Pb²⁺ is added slowly to a mixture containing 0.10 M Cl⁻, 0.10 M I⁻ and 0.10 M SO₄²⁻. Use Ksp values: PbCl₂ = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵; PbI₂ = 8.5 × 10⁻⁹; PbSO₄ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸. Neglect dilution at onset. Which precipitate forms first? Show all three thresholds. (4)</li></ol>`,solution:`<p><b>(a)</b> Add <b>NaCl(aq)</b>: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s). A precipitate identifies Ag⁺; filter and keep the filtrate.<br>Add <b>Na₂SO₄(aq)</b> to that filtrate: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s). Filter again.<br>Add <b>Na₂S(aq)</b> to the new filtrate: Ni²⁺ + S²⁻ → NiS(s).<br>Use (aq) for each ion and (s) for each product. At each step, no precipitate indicates absence of that tested ion under the assumed detection conditions. NaOH with Ni²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Ni(OH)₂(s) is an acceptable final alternative.</p><p><b>(b)</b> PbCl₂: [Pb²⁺] = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵/(0.10)² = <b>1.2 × 10⁻³ M</b>.<br>PbI₂: [Pb²⁺] = 8.5 × 10⁻⁹/(0.10)² = <b>8.5 × 10⁻⁷ M</b>.<br>PbSO₄: [Pb²⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸/0.10 = <b>1.8 × 10⁻⁷ M</b>.<br><b>PbSO₄ forms first</b>: it has the lowest required free [Pb²⁺].</p><p class="rubric">(a) sensible reagent order and compounds 2 + three correct equations 3 + filtration/procedure 1; (b) one mark per threshold 3 + correct first solid and reason 1.</p>`}
];
U3.extra=[
['Foundations','Concentration or solubility?','A student dissolves 0.010 mol of salt in 0.500 L and finds 0.020 M. Has the student measured solubility?','Only if the solution is saturated at a known temperature. Otherwise this is just its concentration.','Do not call every molarity a solubility.'],
['Foundations','A mass conversion','A salt has molar mass 150.0 g/mol and molar solubility 2.40 × 10⁻³ M. What mass dissolves in 250.0 mL?','m = sVMᵣ = (2.40 × 10⁻³)(0.2500)(150.0) = <b>0.0900 g</b>.','Molar solubility → amount dissolved.'],
['Foundations','Two chloride sources','Mix 20.0 mL of 0.100 M MgCl₂ with 30.0 mL of 0.200 M NaCl. Find [Mg²⁺], [Na⁺], and [Cl⁻].','Total volume = 0.0500 L. [Mg²⁺] = 0.00200/0.0500 = <b>0.0400 M</b>. [Na⁺] = 0.00600/0.0500 = <b>0.120 M</b>. Chloride moles = 2(0.00200) + 0.00600 = 0.01000 mol; [Cl⁻] = <b>0.200 M</b>.','Sum moles of a shared ion before dividing.'],
['Curves','Cooling a smaller sample','Salt A has solubility 70 g/100 g water at 80 °C and 25 g/100 g water at 20 °C. A saturated solution in 60 g water is cooled between these temperatures. Find the crystals.','(70 − 25) × 60/100 = <b>27 g</b> crystallizes. Initial dissolved mass = 42 g; final dissolved mass = 15 g.','Scale the difference to water mass.'],
['Curves','Evaporation threshold','At a certain temperature, A dissolves 30 g/100 g water and B dissolves 60 g/100 g water. A mixture has 20 g of each in 100 g water. Ignoring interactions, which forms crystals first as water evaporates?','For A, saturation occurs at water mass = 20 × 100/30 = <b>66.7 g</b>. For B, at 33.3 g. Starting at 100 g and evaporating, you reach 66.7 g first, so <b>A</b> crystallizes first.','First saturation on evaporation is reached while more water remains.'],
['Equations','Iron(III) hydroxide','Write all three equations for FeCl₃(aq) + NaOH(aq).','<b>Formula:</b> FeCl₃(aq) + 3NaOH(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s) + 3NaCl(aq).<br><b>Complete:</b> Fe³⁺ + 3Cl⁻ + 3Na⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₃(s) + 3Na⁺ + 3Cl⁻ (all free ions aq).<br><b>Net:</b> Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s).','Balance atoms and charge, not just symbols.'],
['Equations','No precipitation','Mix KNO₃(aq) and NaCl(aq). Write the net ionic precipitation equation.','<b>No net ionic precipitation reaction.</b> All ions stay aqueous under these conditions; a complete ionic representation would cancel entirely.','Do not invent a solid just because two solutions mix.'],
['Ksp','Given equilibrium ions','A saturated mixture in contact with Ag₂S has [Ag⁺] = 2.0 × 10⁻¹¹ M and [S²⁻] = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ M. Find Ksp.','Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[S²⁻] = (2.0 × 10⁻¹¹)²(3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) = <b>1.2 × 10⁻³¹</b>. Use the given concentrations directly.','Do not force a pure-water ion ratio onto independent equilibrium measurements.'],
['Ksp','AB₂ from s','A hypothetical AB₂ salt has s = 2.00 × 10⁻⁴ M. Find both ion concentrations and Ksp.','[A] = <b>2.00 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>; [B] = <b>4.00 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>. Ksp = 4s³ = <b>3.20 × 10⁻¹¹</b>.','Use the factor 4 and the cube.'],
['Ksp','Hydroxide from Ksp','For Mg(OH)₂, Ksp = 5.6 × 10⁻¹². Find s and [OH⁻] in pure water using the class approximation.','s = ∛(5.6 × 10⁻¹²/4) = 1.1187 × 10⁻⁴ → <b>1.1 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>. [OH⁻] = 2s = <b>2.2 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>.','Molar solubility and hydroxide concentration are different.'],
['Ksp','Different formulas, surprising ranking','Salt AB has Ksp = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰; salt CD₂ has Ksp = 4.0 × 10⁻¹². Which has greater pure-water molar solubility?','AB: s = √(1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) = <b>1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M</b>. CD₂: s = ∛(4.0 × 10⁻¹²/4) = <b>1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M</b>. <b>CD₂</b> is ten times more soluble despite smaller Ksp.','Rank s, not unlike Ksp expressions.'],
['Precipitation','Will dilution prevent precipitation?','Mix 10.0 mL of 1.0 × 10⁻³ M Pb(NO₃)₂ with 40.0 mL of 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ M Na₂SO₄. Ksp(PbSO₄) = 1.8 × 10⁻⁸. Predict precipitation.','[Pb²⁺] = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ M; [SO₄²⁻] = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ M. Q = <b>2.4 × 10⁻⁸ &gt; Ksp</b>; <b>a precipitate forms</b>.','Recompute instead of guessing from dilution alone.'],
['Precipitation','A two-salt competition','Ag⁺ is added to a solution containing 0.010 M Cl⁻ and 0.010 M CrO₄²⁻. Ksp(AgCl) = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰; Ksp(Ag₂CrO₄) = 1.1 × 10⁻¹². Which precipitates first?','AgCl needs [Ag⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰/0.010 = <b>1.8 × 10⁻⁸ M</b>. Ag₂CrO₄ needs [Ag⁺] = √(1.1 × 10⁻¹²/0.010) = <b>1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M</b>. <b>AgCl first</b>.','This is the logic behind the chloride indicator.'],
['Precipitation','Maximum anion concentration','What maximum [CO₃²⁻] can coexist with 0.20 M Mg²⁺ at the saturation threshold? Ksp(MgCO₃) = 6.8 × 10⁻⁶.','[CO₃²⁻] = Ksp/[Mg²⁺] = 6.8 × 10⁻⁶/0.20 = <b>3.4 × 10⁻⁵ M</b>. Above this, Q exceeds Ksp.','Use equality to find the boundary.'],
['Separation','Two-ion procedure','A solution may contain Ag⁺ and Al³⁺. Choose a reagent order and give equations.','Add <b>NaCl(aq)</b>: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s). Filter. Add <b>NaOH(aq)</b> carefully to the filtrate: Al³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Al(OH)₃(s). Under the class-table model this identifies Al³⁺; avoid a large excess of strong base because aluminum hydroxide is amphoteric.','Reagent order must preserve selectivity.'],
['Separation','Mass of precipitate','A 25.0 mL sample gives 0.583 g dry BaSO₄ after excess sulfate is added. Molar mass BaSO₄ = 233.4 g/mol. Find original [Ba²⁺].','n(BaSO₄) = 0.583/233.4 = 0.0024979 mol = n(Ba²⁺). [Ba²⁺] = 0.0024979/0.0250 = <b>0.0999 M</b>.','Precipitate mass → moles → original ion concentration; #37 skill.'],
['Titration','A fresh titration','A 25.00 mL aliquot requires 12.60 mL of 0.0500 M AgNO₃. The aliquot came from a 5.00× dilution. Find chloride concentration before dilution.','n(Cl⁻) = (0.0500)(0.01260) = 6.30 × 10⁻⁴ mol. Diluted concentration = 6.30 × 10⁻⁴/0.02500 = 0.0252 M. Original = 5.00 × 0.0252 = <b>0.126 M</b>.','Divide by aliquot volume, then reverse the dilution.'],
['Titration','Water in the flask','After pipetting the sample, a student adds 20 mL of distilled water to wash down the flask walls. Does the required titre change?','<b>No</b>, assuming no chloride is lost or added. Chloride moles are unchanged. Calculate the original aliquot concentration using the aliquot volume, not the later total flask volume.','Titration measures amount, then you recover the concentration.'],
['Hard water','Selective versus complete removal','Explain the difference between Na₂SO₄ and Na₂CO₃ when treating a Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ mixture.','Sulfate can precipitate <b>CaSO₄</b> but leaves soluble MgSO₄. Carbonate can precipitate <b>both CaCO₃ and MgCO₃</b>. Amounts and concentrations must be sufficient to exceed each Ksp.','The two main hard-water MC patterns.'],
['Hard water','Why sodium is useful','Why use Na₂CO₃ rather than CaCO₃(s) as a source of carbonate for precipitation softening?','Na₂CO₃ is soluble and supplies carbonate ions. CaCO₃ has low solubility and supplies little free carbonate; calcium is itself a hardness ion.','Choose a soluble reagent that does not introduce the ion you want to remove.']
].map((x,i)=>({id:'e'+(i+1),tag:x[0],title:x[1],q:x[2],solution:x[3],why:x[4]}));
U3.recall=[
['What do you do before every Ksp calculation?','Write and balance the dissolving equilibrium, then write the Ksp expression.'],
['When is s equal to the cation concentration?','When one cation is produced per formula unit and that cation initially comes only from dissolving this salt.'],
['What is the first action when two solutions are mixed?','Find the total volume and dilute each relevant ion, including all stoichiometric factors.'],
['What does Q > Ksp mean?','The mixture has an excess of dissolved ions relative to saturation; net precipitation is favoured.'],
['What do you compare for “which precipitates first”?','The threshold concentration of the same ion being added for each possible solid. Smallest threshold wins.'],
['Which species are never split in a complete ionic equation?','Pure solids, liquids, gases, and molecular/weakly ionizing species. Only split the appropriate dissolved strong electrolytes.'],
['What separates a formula equation from a net ionic equation?','Formula equations use complete compound formulas; net ionic equations show only the ions that change and the resulting product.'],
['What is essential between qualitative-analysis steps?','Filter the precipitate and keep the filtrate for the next selective test.'],
['What is the chloride titration mole ratio?','One Ag⁺ reacts with one Cl⁻ to form AgCl.'],
['What is the indicator precipitate, and what is the main precipitate?','Indicator endpoint: red/orange Ag₂CrO₄. Main chloride reaction: white AgCl.'],
['How do you recover the original sample concentration after a 10× dilution?','Multiply the measured diluted-sample concentration by 10.'],
['What two ions cause the hard-water questions in your course?','Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺. Carbonate can remove both; sulfate can selectively remove calcium under suitable conditions.'],
['Does adding a common ion change Ksp?','No, at fixed temperature. It reduces the salt’s solubility, changes equilibrium concentrations, and may form more solid.'],
['What is the danger in reading a solubility curve?','Confusing g per 100 g water with g/L solution, or forgetting to scale to the actual water mass.'],
['How should you round multi-step calculations?','Keep guard digits throughout; round the final answer using the limiting significant figures. Buret subtraction uses decimal-place precision.']
];
